045417] [17592186262469 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262469 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262469 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262469 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bdb5524e-d28d-538f-a91e-26dfa96b9bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffc75d90-c023-5fb9-a940-576505f511c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Q(4,-2,8)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-3,6,2⟩=-8$, find the shortest distance from $P(3,-4,2)$ to $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0498861d-e4e1-5581-aee5-353d6a78f4f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262941 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-2,8⟩-⟨3,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-3,6,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 6+6\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-3+12+12|}{\\sqrt{9+36+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|21|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Q(3,-4,5)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-2,4,-4⟩=-42$, find the shortest distance from $P(1,-5,2)$ to $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c91ce04-7e86-5b0f-8e36-5b36844cedf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262942 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,5⟩-⟨1,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-2,4,-4⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+4-12|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,3,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-x+2y+2z=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fc468d7-8c5a-51cb-a422-a6e8deebdd30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262962 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-2$ and $y=1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2y+2z & =8 \\\\ -1(-2)+2(1)+2z & =8 \\\\ 2z & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,1,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,1,2⟩-⟨-2,3,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-1,2,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0-4+16|}{\\sqrt{1+4+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312239 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312239 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312239 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23c37932-b8d1-516f-abf3-f1748834a7af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312486 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{48}{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312486 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312486 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f3b0406-2681-5fe8-a9d6-e948d2f27a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312817 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{43}{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b7e35c1-be2d-55da-bd15-0ac93254cb95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313242 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313242 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313242 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1272a18-c467-54d3-9f71-ae126c7eade1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313752 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{11\\sqrt{26}}{13}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313752 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313752 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1afee6d-ef0d-5bae-8b1d-2c7e13dcdbb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186313242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186313752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186312817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c0d43f73-b51f-5e33-b3f5-c33dee8bb5b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Q(2,4,1)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-6,2,9⟩=5$, find the shortest distance from $P(6,-1,0)$ to $Π.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "245878e4-0685-5305-b361-ba9fd660a2d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315010 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,4,1⟩-⟨6,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-6,2,9⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot (-6)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 9|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|24+10+9|}{\\sqrt{36+4+81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|43|}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
